Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "opponent next-possession points per possession (PPP) following a given offensive play type",
    "defensive aftershock: magnitude and between-type variation of opponent PPP after specific play types",
    "AVOID labels driven by opponent post-play PPP",
    "own-sequence dependence (lagged play-type effects on own efficiency)",
    "play-type labeling quality (foul leakage, misclassification) and its impact on sequence analyses"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "Are observed differences in opponent next-possession PPP across play types large enough and reliable enough to inform AVOID labels?",
    "What is the effect size and practical significance for each play type, and does the between-type variance exceed plausible confound?",
    "How does play-type labeling error (especially foul leakage for DRIVE and OTHER) bias the estimated opponent PPP differences?",
    "What sample sizes are required to achieve adequate power for detecting practically meaningful AVOID-level differences, given the small between-type spread seen in prior aftershock estimates?",
    "How do own-sequence lags (previous own play type, outcome, and spacing) modulate opponent next-possession PPP, and can they be separated from general dead-ball/score effects?",
    "How should an AVOID label be operationally defined to avoid lookahead leakage and outcome-derived label circularity?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF07",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "After the opponent runs play type X, the next offensive play type Y scores above Y's unconditional H2 baseline (sequential pair premium). All 8 play types exhibit a defensive aftershock: opponent next-possession PPP is lower than unconditional opponent PPP after a successful own possession, with effects ranging from +0.119 (CUT) to +0.135 (TRANSITION) PPP points below a baseline of 1.206. Both results replicate in all-games and playoff subsets. Own-next effects were treated as exploratory (~4% of pairs)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "A play-type labeling audit found that 36% of possessions labeled OTHER end in fouls, inflating OTHER PPP. DRIVE shows foul leakage (PPP 12 pp below a foul benchmark). PNR has a 22% foul rate. Only 0.7% of possessions map to a real play type via initiation_type. These labeling artifacts can produce spurious play-type PPP differences."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "what_failed": "Play-type PPP difference analysis relying on un-audited labels",
      "why": "C_artifact: apparent PPP differences for OTHER and DRIVE were driven by foul-related labeling flaws rather than genuine efficiency. Initiation_type mapping covers only 0.7% of possessions, indicating deep label provenance problems."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "A robust defensive aftershock exists: opponent PPP after a successful own possession is about 0.12-0.13 points lower than the unconditional opponent baseline of ~1.21 PPP.",
    "The effect is consistent across all eight major play types, with a spread of only ~0.016 PPP between the smallest and largest aftershock.",
    "Large-sample analyses (n ~ 458k opponent possessions) confirm the effect is not a small-sample artifact, though some sub-group (team-pair) cells are sparse.",
    "Play-type labeling is contaminated by foul-related misclassification (especially OTHER and DRIVE), which can inflate PPP and distort comparisons."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether the tiny between-play-type spread in aftershock (range <0.02 PPP) is large enough to justify a practical AVOID label.",
    "AVOID labels themselves: no prior study operationalised or validated a decision rule based on opponent next-possession PPP.",
    "The role of own-sequence lags: only exploratory treatment exists, so their separate contribution and potential confound with team-level rhythm or game-state are unquantified.",
    "Causal attribution: the aftershock may be a generic dead-ball or score-differential effect rather than a play-type-specific defensive response.",
    "Generalizability to non-success possessions or to situations where play-type labels are noisy (the audit found issues for certain types)."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question is testable in principle because the data contain the necessary sequential possession structure, play-type labels, and opponent PPP outcomes.",
    "Given the small between-type aftershock spread found in SF07, a test of AVOID-label-worthiness based solely on opponent next-possession PPP is likely underpowered for detecting practically large differences unless very large samples are used, and even then the differences may be too small to be actionable.",
    "Play-type labeling corruption (SF-CHT-025) means that a direct analysis without careful filtering (e.g., excluding OTHER, adjusting DRIVE for foul leakage, or using only confirmed initiation_type mappings) could yield misleading AVOID signals.",
    "The AVOID label is an analyst-defined outcome that must be specified a priori; otherwise the study risks circularity (choosing the threshold that gives desired result).",
    "Own-sequence lags are likely confounded with possession-outcome (score/miss/turnover) and end-of-quarter effects, requiring careful stratification."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "No evidence on what magnitude of opponent PPP difference coaches or analysts consider large enough for an AVOID label; the smallest meaningful effect could be larger than the between-type spread seen.",
    "The defensive aftershock measured in SF07 is post-success; the question does not specify whether own possession success/failure is a condition.",
    "The play-type labeling pipeline's current state (2025-26) is unknown; improvements or regressions from the audited state (2024?) are undocumented.",
    "The extent to which own-sequence lag effects are independent of opponent defensive adjustments vs. simple time-series autocorrelation in team performance.",
    "Whether AVOID labels derived from opponent PPP would replicate across seasons or be overfit to a particular dataset."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "Without history, one might assume large play-type-specific differences in opponent next-possession PPP exist and can be straightforwardly turned into AVOID labels. The historical evidence shows that (1) a uniform defensive aftershock does exist across play types, but (2) the between-type spread is very small (≤0.016 PPP), making it unlikely to support strong AVOID distinctions, and (3) play-type labeling artifacts (foul leakage) can create spurious differences. This shifts the decision from a simple proceed to a cautious proceed-with-changes: the test must be scoped to detect meaningfully large (not just statistically significant) differences, must control for labeling quality, and must pre-commit to AVOID thresholds."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.7,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Some play-type pairs and team-specific cells have very small sample sizes, limiting precise estimation of play-type-level opponent PPP differences."
      },
      {
        "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
        "text": "The AVOID label is not directly observed; it must be defined post-hoc based on opponent PPP differences, risking circularity if the threshold is not pre-specified."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Play-type labels have known foul-related misclassification (OTHER/DRIVE) that could bias opponent PPP estimates."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Game-state factors (score margin, clock, timeouts, end-of-quarter dead balls) plausibly drive both play-type selection and subsequent opponent PPP, confounding the causal link."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF07",
      "FAM:SF-CHT-025"
    ],
    "summary": "SF07 establishes that opponent PPP drops after a successful own possession for all main play types, with a very tight range (~0.016 PPP). SF-CHT-025 demonstrates that certain play-type labels are contaminated by foul artifacts. Together they partially address the existence and magnitude of opponent next-possession PPP effects but not the specific AVOID-label decision framework or own-sequence lag structure."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Between-play-type aftershock differences are small (max ~0.016 PPP). Detecting a difference of, say, 0.02 PPP with 80% power would require an extremely large sample, possibly exceeding available data when split by play type, team, and context."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "End-of-quarter dead-ball scenarios, score-dependent tactical changes, and lineup strength all correlate with both play-type choice and subsequent opponent performance, masking or inflating play-type-specific effects."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Foul leakage in DRIVE and OTHER labels (and the low mapping rate of initiation_type) means that unadjusted play-type assignments may misattribute opponent PPP effects."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "AVOID classification will be derived from the very opponent PPP data used to test it, creating an outcome-derived label circularity unless the threshold is predetermined and fixed from prior evidence."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "Own-sequence lag analyses can inadvertently include future information (e.g., using subsequent quarter outcomes to condition the sequence) if not implemented with strict temporal cutoffs."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Comparing many play types, team contexts, and lag structures will multiply the chance of false positive AVOID labels."
    },
    {
      "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
      "text": "The labeling pipeline and rule changes (e.g., foul interpretation) may shift between seasons, limiting the external validity of AVOID rules learned on 2022-25 data when applied to 2025-26."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.15,
    "rejected": 0.15,
    "untestable": 0.30,
    "artifact": 0.15,
    "uncertain": 0.25
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "Average difference in opponent next-possession PPP between a reference play type and each other play type, conditional on own possession outcome (success/failure), possession context (score margin ≤5, quarter 2-4, non-dead-ball), and team defensive strength. For each play type, the deviation from the reference type's opponent PPP will be compared to a pre-specified AVOID threshold (e.g., ≥0.05 PPP above the lowest type).",
    "unit": "A possession pair: (own possession with play type X, immediately following opponent possession).",
    "sample": "All regular-season and playoff games from 2022-23 through 2025-26 where the play-type label is from the validated subset (excluding OTHER; DRIVE adjusted for foul leakage or flagged). Minimum 200 possessions per play type per season after exclusions. Split 80/20 temporal validation (2022-25 train, 2025-26 test).",
    "power": "Target 80% power to detect an opponent PPP difference of 0.05 points between the highest- and lowest-aftershock play type at α=0.05, using a balanced one-way ANOVA. Required n ≈ 2500 opponent possessions per play type. The study will document actual achieved power based on observed sample sizes.",
    "controls": "Fixed effects for: season, home/away, own possession outcome (made/miss/turnover), point differential group (-3 to +3, -10 to -2, +2 to +10, trailing/leading double digit), period (2,3,4 excluding last 2 minutes of each), timeouts remaining, defensive efficiency of the opposing team (decile). Random intercepts for team and opponent.",
    "falsification": "1. Test that the overall average opponent PPP is identical to prior baseline (1.206) on held-out data; 2. Check that play-type aftershock rank-order is consistent across seasons using rank correlation; 3. Verify that after random permutation of play-type labels, the between-type variance drops to chance levels; 4. Demonstrate that the AVOID-labeled types identified in train data fail to produce an opponent PPP advantage in the test set by more than a trivial margin (equivalence test).",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-register a single primary contrast: whether the range of play-type opponent PPP after adjustment exceeds 0.05 PPP. Secondary comparisons of individual types to reference will use Holm-Bonferroni correction. Lag-order interactions (own sequence) will be tested only as an exploratory subgroup.",
    "decision_rule": "A play type is flagged for AVOID consideration if its adjusted opponent PPP is ≥0.05 higher than the type with the lowest opponent PPP, and this difference is statistically significant at adjusted p<0.05 in the training sample and replicates directionally in the test sample (one-sided p<0.10). The AVOID label is not considered confirmed unless both criteria hold and the falsification checks pass.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 (full data as available on 2026-06-19), per-game play-by-play processed through the standard pipeline as of that date. Play-type labels taken from the initiation graph database, with documented filtering for foul-related contamination. Exact SQL/view definitions and pipeline commit hash to be recorded."
  }
}

